From 92dff5f7bdbea5d8b7c74a0469e406789c24f3f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 18:31:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] goal loop: bound the machine-interrupt re-arm so a wedged
 route cannot spin
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes a gap patch 0007 opened. A turn the PROVIDER cut short costs no budget
-- correctly, since it produced no work -- so the re-arm would retry a
permanently wedged route forever without ever reaching max_turns. That is a
better failure than the silent stall it replaced, and it is still a spin.

After max_consecutive_machine_interrupts (default 12, configurable) the route
is broken rather than busy, so the goal pauses -- with a TRANSPORT-shaped
reason, which is the class an auto-resume supervisor WILL pick back up once
the route answers. Bounded, not abandoned. 12 is generous deliberately: the
measured stalls here are 33-137 s and recover on the next attempt, so the
bound must only trip when the route is genuinely gone.  [measured]

The streak is cleared by any turn that produced real work, and by
`/goal resume`. That second clear is the flap guard: without it a resumed goal
comes back AT the threshold and the first machine interrupt re-pauses it,
which makes both the resume command and the supervisor useless against
exactly the failure they exist for.

Requires patch 0007 -- it extends the provenance classification that patch
introduces.

BASE: the tree left by patch 0008 (see BASELINE.md).

